UID:624760780e9f73ed@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260915T211923Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor investigation finds Ch
 icago construction employer exposed workers to continued safety hazards\, 
 proposes $265K in penalties
DESCRIPTION:CHICAGO – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited a Calumet C
 ity-based residential construction and carpentry employer after two separa
 te investigations into its continued failure to provide fall protection fo
 r workers. The department’s Occupational Safety and Health Administrati
 on investigated two worksites operated by Martin Araujo – doing business
  as Araujo Construction Corp. – and found that carpenters and general co
 nstruction laborers were installing roof rafters and roof sheeting without
  fall protection\, exposing them to falls of up to 25 feet. OSHA cited Ar
 aujo Construction with two willful violations for not providing fall prote
 ction\, while working from heights during residential construction at the 
 two worksites. OSHA also cited the employer with nine repeat safety violat
 ions for failing to provide head and eye protection\, lacking handrails on
  stairways\, and failing to make sure ladders extended three feet above ac
 cess areas. Additionally\, OSHA issued a serious violation to Araujo Const
 ruction for not having safe access to a platform\, and an other-than-serio
 us violation for lacking training certifications. OSHA proposed $265\,868
  in combined penalties.Martin Araujo has 15 business days from receipt of 
 its citations and penalties to comply\, request an informal conference wit
 h OSHA’s area director\, or contest the findings before the independent 
 Occupational Safety and Health Review Commission. Please check the OSHA es
 tablishment search page periodically for any changes in the inspection or 
 penalty status.  Under the Occupational Safety and Health Act of 1970\, 
 employers are responsible for providing safe and healthful workplaces for 
 their employees. OSHA’s role is to ensure these conditions for America
 ’s working men and women by setting and enforcing standards\, and provid
 ing training\, education and assistance. See OSHA’s website for more inf
 ormation.\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20260915
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20260915
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:56a43224d6e6a8f8@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260914T214927Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] #229. Bar Admission Rule 11 candidate employe
 d by a Maine law firm.
DESCRIPTION:Skip First Level Navigation\nSkip All Navigation\nBoard of Ove
 rseers of the Bar\nContact Us\n|\nSitemap\nHome\nContact Us\nEmployment\nM
 yBoardAccess\nHome\n→\nAttorney Services\n→\nEthics Opinions\n→ Opin
 ion\nAbout the Board\nAttorney Directory\nAttorney Services\nAttorney Logi
 n (MyBoardAccess)\nRegistration\nContinuing Legal Education\nEthics Opinio
 ns\nBar Counsel Notes\nEthics Helpline\nGAL Review Board\nHow to File a Co
 mplaint\nRegulation\nDecisions\, Awards and Hearing Schedule\nLegal Resour
 ces\nOpinion #229. Bar Admission Rule 11 candidate employed by a Maine law
  firm.\nIssued by the Professional Ethics Commission\nDate Issued: August 
 31\, 2026\nMaine Professional Ethics Commission Formal Opinion #229: Augus
 t 2026\nThe Professional Ethics Commission was presented with a question r
 egarding the application of Maine Rule of Professional Conduct 5.5(c) with
  respect to an attorney employed by a law firm under the following circums
 tances:\nAn attorney licensed and in good standing before the bar of anoth
 er state who has been hired by a firm in Maine\, moved to Maine\, is worki
 ng for that firm\, and has applied for admission to the Maine Bar by motio
 n pursuant to Bar Admission Rule 11\, but has not yet been admitted. \n\nT
 his opinion examines this specific question in light of M.R. Prof. Conduct
  5.5(c)\, its relationship to ABA Model Rule 5.5(c)\, in the context of gu
 idance from Maine Professional Ethics Opinion No. 189 (2005).\nRules\, Sta
 tutes and Authorities\nABA Model Rule 5.5(c)\nABA Comm'n on Multijurisdict
 ional Prac.\, Rept. 201B\nM. Bar Admission R. 11\nMaine Professional Ethic
 s Opinion 189\nM.R. Civ. P. 89(b)\nM.R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(c) and (d)\n4 M.
 R.S.  802\n4 M.R.S.  805-A\n4 M.R.S.  807\nDiscussion\nI. Maine Statute an
 d the Maine Rules of Professional Conduct Prohibit the Unauthorized Practi
 ce of Law.\nBy statute and rule\, Maine prohibits the "unauthorized practi
 ce of law."\n1\n4 M.R.S.  807\, M.R. Prof. Conduct 5.5.  Attorneys from ot
 her jurisdictions may be admitted on motion to try cases in Maine pursuant
  to 4 M.R.\nhttp://www.mebaroverseers.org/attorney_services/opinion.html?i
 d=13362285
URL:http://www.mebaroverseers.org/attorney_services/opinion.html?id=133622
 85
